<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  VM live test: upload to a SeaweedFS (S3) bucket, whole object in one PUT.

  Byte for byte the dialog of vm-minio.xml and vm-rustfs.xml, pointed at a THIRD S3 implementation, and
  anonymous for the same reason they are: this fixture proves the UNAUTHENTICATED dialog on its own, so
  that vm-seaweedfs-sigv4.xml's verdict is about the signature and nothing else. setup_seaweedfs.sh
  grants the anonymous identity writes under orc-upload/ of the orc bucket and nothing else - no read,
  no list. Anyone holding this file can write there, which is why the bucket is versioned, so an
  overwrite adds a version instead of destroying a collection, and why a real deployment of this shape
  must seal its output (CMS encryption) rather than trust the bucket.

  The limits vm-minio.xml sets out apply here identically, because they are properties of S3 and of what
  a <rest> step can express, not of any one server:

  - NO CHUNKING. S3 splits a large object with multipart upload, which needs the UploadId out of a
    POST ?uploads XML response BODY - this engine captures response headers only - and a final request
    whose body is XML built from every part's ETag. So there is no seaweedfs-chunked companion to this
    fixture, and an object is capped at S3's 5 GiB single-PUT limit.
  - NO RESUME. A PUT replaces an object rather than appending, so the HEAD -> offset pattern of
    rest-resume.xml would upload the tail as the whole new object.

  SeaweedFS serves the sink's own server.crt on 8454, so the committed pin applies unchanged and nothing
  here needs insecure="true": the transport is verified even though the destination is anonymous.
-->
<dfir-orc>
    <upload method="REST" server="%ORC_UPLOAD_SERVER%"
            https_security="tls1.3, tls1.2" operation="copy"
            server_cert_pin="">
        <rest http_method="PUT" path="/orc/orc-upload/{upload.filename}" body="{upload.local_path}"/>
    </upload>
</dfir-orc>
